title: dllhost.exe Masquerade From Outside Windows Directories (SQL Server Gh0st RAT campaign)
id: 47ed99fa-fbfd-485e-a7d7-080a02279ade
status: experimental
description: >
    Detects dllhost.exe executing from a path outside the Windows system
    directories, the masquerade used by the October 2026 campaign that
    brute-forces internet-facing Microsoft SQL Server logins and runs
    attacker-supplied T-SQL to deploy a payload. The stage-one loader is a
    UPX-packed Go binary named dllhost.exe that embeds the Naabu v2 port
    scanner, retrieves its target range from
    hxxp://site.vietnam852[.]com:88/ip.php, then installs Gh0st RAT and a
    Monero miner. The legitimate COM+ surrogate host only runs from
    System32/SysWOW64, so an instance in a temp, data or application directory
    on a database host is the hunting signal. Derived from published campaign
    reporting - not validated against a live sample.
references:
    - https://gbhackers.com/microsoft-sql-servers-hijacked/
    - https://www.techfinitive.com/weak-passwords-and-sql-used-in-gh0st-rat-attack-against-microsoft-users/
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/11/13
tags:
    - attack.t1036
    - attack.t1059
    - attack.t1105
logsource:
    category: process_creation
    product: windows
detection:
    selection_img:
        Image|endswith: '\dllhost.exe'
    filter_system:
        Image|startswith:
            - 'C:\Windows\System32\'
            - 'C:\Windows\SysWOW64\'
    condition: selection_img and not filter_system
falsepositives:
    - Third-party software shipping or installing its own copy of a COM+ surrogate host outside the Windows directories
    - Portable application bundles and installers that stage a dllhost.exe copy in their own program directory
level: high
